  "query_preview": "..\nToday and tonight: Severe thunderstorms that tracked through the\nregion on Thursday have exited the region. Yet a moist air mass\ncombined with an approaching upper trough from the west will\nresult in a favorable environment for additional shower and\nthunderstorm activity today, but not as intense as Thursday. There\nare expected to be two areas of focus for showers and\nthunderstorms today. One being along the East Slopes of the\nCascades into the Okanogan Highlands as a mid level moisture\nboundary stalls over these areas this morning providing an added\nboost to boundary layer moisture and afternoon instability. A\nsecond area is over the Idaho Panhandle where an instability axis\nlingers. Unlike surface based CAPE as high as 2000-3000 J/KG that\nwas observed on Thursday, values Friday will be closer to 500-1000\nJ/KG. These values however combined with 0-6km shear values of\n20-30 kts has the potential for a few stronger storms to develop.\nThe approaching trough today will help increase the west-southwest\nwinds over the region allowing for cooler temperatures. Winds will\nbe strongest out over the Columbia Basin and Wenatchee area with\nsustained west to southwest winds 15 to 25 mph with gusts of 25-35\nmph. Tonight the cooler, stable air will continue to move into the\nregion resulting in showers decreasing during the evening hours.\n\nSaturday through Monday: The low that has been over central\nCalifornia and Nevada will finally eject northeast, moving into\nMontana and stalling once again. Moisture wrapping around the back\nside of the low will result in a chance for showers over the Idaho\nPanhandle, mainly on Sunday and Monday. There is a 20-30 percent\nchance of a few of these showers spilling into NE Washington as\nwell. Cloud cover over the ID Panhandle will result in high\ntemperatures each day in the mid to upper 60s, while high\ntemperatures in Central Washington warm into the 70s with\nincreased sunshine.\n\nTuesday through Thursday: A large low in the Gulf of Alaska brings\na zonal flow into the region. Embedded disturbances within the\nwesterly flow will result in a chance of showers mainly over the\nmountains. There is also a 20 percent chance of thunderstorms over\nthe northern mountains Wednesday afternoon. Temperatures are\nprojected to be slightly above normal, with highs in the mid 70s\nto low 80s. JW",
